{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1719,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In this phone, there are nearly 100 million\ntransistors, in this computer there's over"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5939,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a billion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6939,
    "dDurationMs": 6051,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The transistor is in virtually every electronic\ndevice we use: TV's, radios, Tamagotchis."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12990,
    "dDurationMs": 1500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But how does it work?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14490,
    "dDurationMs": 2850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well the basic principle is actually incredibly\nsimple."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17340,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It works just like this switch, so it controls\nthe flow of electric current."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22400,
    "dDurationMs": 6539,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It can be off, so you could call that the\nzero state or it could be on, the one state."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28939,
    "dDurationMs": 5241,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this is how all of our information is\nnow stored and processed, in zeros and ones,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34180,
    "dDurationMs": 2310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "little bits of electric current."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36490,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But unlike this switch, a transistor doesn't\nhave any moving parts."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40870,
    "dDurationMs": 2270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it also doesn't require a human controller."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43140,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Furthermore, it can be switched on and off\nmuch more quickly than I can flick this switch."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48300,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And finally, and most importantly it is incredibly\ntiny."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52800,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well this is all thanks to the miracle of\nsemiconductors or rather I should say the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58360,
    "dDurationMs": 2500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "science of semiconductors."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60860,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Pure silicon is a semiconductor, which means\nit conducts electric current better than insulators"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66230,
    "dDurationMs": 2080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but not as well as metals."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68310,
    "dDurationMs": 6430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is because an atom of silicon has four\nelectrons in its outermost or valence shell."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74740,
    "dDurationMs": 3180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This allows it to form bonds with its four\nnearest neighbours,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77920,
    "dDurationMs": 1160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hidey ho there!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79080,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "G'day\nWasaaaaap!?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80080,
    "dDurationMs": 1980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So it forms a tetrahedral crystal."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82060,
    "dDurationMs": 5610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But since all these electrons are stuck in\nbonds, few ever get enough energy to escape"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87670,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their bonds and travel through the lattice."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91330,
    "dDurationMs": 5740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So having a small number of mobile charges\nis what makes silicon a semi-conductor."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97070,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now this wouldn't be all that useful without\na semiconductor's secret weapon -- doping."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102590,
    "dDurationMs": 4459,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You've probably heard of doping, it's when\nyou inject a foreign substance in order to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107049,
    "dDurationMs": 2201,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "improve performance."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109250,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yeah it's actually just like that, except\non the atomic level."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113720,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are two types of doping called n-type\nand p-type."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117600,
    "dDurationMs": 4770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To make n-type semiconductor, you take pure\nsilicon and inject a small amount of an element"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122370,
    "dDurationMs": 3730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with 5 valence electrons,\nlike Phosphorous."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126100,
    "dDurationMs": 4170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is useful because Phosphorous is similar\nenough to silicon that it can fit into the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130270,
    "dDurationMs": 3700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lattice, but it brings with it an extra electron."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133970,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So this means now the semiconductor has more\nmobile charges and so it conducts current"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138670,
    "dDurationMs": 1230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "better."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139900,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In p-type doping, an element with only three\nvalence electrons is added to the lattice."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 144620,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like Boron."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145620,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now this creates a 'hole' - a place where\nthere should be an electron, but there isn't."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150620,
    "dDurationMs": 4729,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this still increases the conductivity\nof the silicon because electrons can move"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155349,
    "dDurationMs": 1381,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156730,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now although it is electrons that are moving,\nwe like to talk about the holes moving around"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160920,
    "dDurationMs": 2570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "-- because there's far fewer of them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163490,
    "dDurationMs": 5079,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now since the hole is the lack of an electron,\nit actually acts as a positive charge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168569,
    "dDurationMs": 3091,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this is why p-type semiconductor is actually\ncalled p-type."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171660,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The p stands for positive - it's positive\ncharges, these holes, which are moving and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176600,
    "dDurationMs": 1490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "conducting the current."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178090,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now it's a common misconception that n-type\nsemiconductors are negatively charged and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182310,
    "dDurationMs": 2040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "p-type semiconductors are positively charged."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184350,
    "dDurationMs": 4180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's not true, they are both neutral because\nthey have the same number of electrons and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188530,
    "dDurationMs": 1490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "protons inside them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190020,
    "dDurationMs": 4859,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The n and the p actually just refer to the\nsign of charge that can move within them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194879,
    "dDurationMs": 4321,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So in n-type, it's negative electrons which\ncan move, and in p-type"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199200,
    "dDurationMs": 2250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's a positive hole that moves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201450,
    "dDurationMs": 1740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But they're both neutral!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 203190,
    "dDurationMs": 4310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A transistor is made with both n-type and\np-type semiconductors."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207500,
    "dDurationMs": 4810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A common configuration has n on the ends with\np in the middle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212310,
    "dDurationMs": 4810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just like a switch a transistor has an electrical\ncontact at each end and these are called the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217120,
    "dDurationMs": 1930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "source and the drain."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219050,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But instead of a mechanical switch, there\nis a third electrical contact called the gate,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223570,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is insulated from the semiconductor\nby an oxide layer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227410,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When a transistor is made, the n and p-types\ndon't keep to themselves -- electrons actually"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233050,
    "dDurationMs": 4769,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "diffuse from the n-type, where there are more\nof them into the p-type"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237819,
    "dDurationMs": 1961,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to fill the holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 239780,
    "dDurationMs": 2590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This creates something called the depletion\nlayer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242370,
    "dDurationMs": 1619,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What's been depleted?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 243989,
    "dDurationMs": 1631,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Charges that can move."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245620,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are no more free electrons in the n-type\n-- why?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 249700,
    "dDurationMs": 2649,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because they've filled the holes in the p-type."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252349,
    "dDurationMs": 3521,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now this makes the p-type negative thanks\nto the added electrons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255870,
    "dDurationMs": 4070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this is important because the p-type will\nnow repel any electrons that try to come across"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 259940,
    "dDurationMs": 1299,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the n-type."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261239,
    "dDurationMs": 4361,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the depletion layer actually acts as a\nbarrier, preventing the flow of electric current"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265600,
    "dDurationMs": 1390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through the transistor."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266990,
    "dDurationMs": 3929,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So right now the transistor is off, it's like\nan open switch, it's in the zero state."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270919,
    "dDurationMs": 5011,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To turn it on, you have to apply a small positive\nvoltage to the gate."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 275930,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This attracts the electrons over and overcomes\nthat repulsion from the depletion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280630,
    "dDurationMs": 4300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It actually shrinks the depletion layer so\nthat electrons can move through and form a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284930,
    "dDurationMs": 1549,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "conducting channel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286479,
    "dDurationMs": 4410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the transistor is now on, it's in the one\nstate."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290889,
    "dDurationMs": 4541,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is remarkable because just by exploiting\nthe properties of a crystal we've been able"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 295430,
    "dDurationMs": 4370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to create a switch that doesn't have any moving\nparts, that can be turned on and off very"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299800,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quickly just with a voltage, and most importantly\nit can be made tiny."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305400,
    "dDurationMs": 6650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Transistors today are only about 22nm wide,\nwhich means they are only about 50 atoms across."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312050,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But to keep up with Moore's law, they're going\nto have to keep getting smaller."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 315850,
    "dDurationMs": 4319,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Moore's Law states that every two years the\nnumber of transistors on a chip should double."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320169,
    "dDurationMs": 5801,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And there is a limit, as those terminals get\ncloser and closer together, quantum effects"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 325970,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "become more significant and electrons can\nactually tunnel from one side to the other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330570,
    "dDurationMs": 4129,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So you may not be able to make a barrier high\nenough to stop them from flowing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 334699,
    "dDurationMs": 5151,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now this will be a real problem for the future\nof transistors, but we'll probably only face"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 339850,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that another ten years down the track."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342190,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So until then transistors, the way we know\nthem, are going to keep getting better."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 346710,
    "dDurationMs": 6670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Once you have let's say three hundred of these\nqubits, then you have like two to the three"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353380,
    "dDurationMs": 2700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hundred classical bits."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356080,
    "dDurationMs": 2359,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which is as many particles as there are in\nthe universe."
    } ]
  } ]
}
